Task 1: Launch VM from the Web Console
Subtask 1.1: Navigate to Virtualization Dashboard
Access the Virtualization Section

In the OpenShift web console, click on Virtualization in the left navigation menu
You should see the virtualization dashboard with options for VirtualMachines, Templates, and InstanceTypes
Explore the Dashboard

Take note of the different tabs: VirtualMachines, Templates, InstanceTypes
Observe any existing virtual machines (if any)
Check the status indicators and resource usage displays
Subtask 1.2: Create a New Virtual Machine
Start VM Creation Process

Click on the VirtualMachines tab
Click the Create button
Select From template option
Choose VM Template

Select a Linux-based template (e.g., Fedora or CentOS)
Click Quick create VirtualMachine for simplified setup
Alternatively, click Customize VirtualMachine for advanced options
Configure Basic VM Settings

# Example VM configuration (if using YAML view)
apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachine
metadata:
  name: lab4-test-vm
  namespace: default
spec:
  running: false
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        kubevirt.io/vm: lab4-test-vm
    spec:
      domain:
        cpu:
          cores: 1
        devices:
          disks:
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: rootdisk
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: cloudinitdisk
          interfaces:
          - masquerade: {}
            name: default
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: 1Gi
      networks:
      - name: default
        pod: {}
      volumes:
      - dataVolume:
          name: lab4-test-vm
        name: rootdisk
      - cloudInitNoCloud:
          userData: |
            #cloud-config
            user: fedora
            password: fedora
            chpasswd: { expire: False }
        name: cloudinitdisk
Set VM Parameters

Name: lab4-test-vm
Memory: 1 GiB
CPU: 1 core
Storage: 10 GiB
Operating System: Fedora (or available Linux distribution)
Subtask 1.3: Deploy the Virtual Machine
Review Configuration

Verify all settings are correct
Check resource allocations
Ensure network configuration is appropriate
Create the VM

Click Create VirtualMachine
Wait for the VM to be created (status will show as Stopped)
Note the VM appears in the VirtualMachines list
Start the Virtual Machine

Click on your newly created VM name
Click the Actions dropdown
Select Start VirtualMachine
Wait for status to change to Running
